<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="7451DB08-3678-4263-BF51-AB5D88E6E93C" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerSampleDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        The SchedulerConfiguration project shows a .NET WinForms application that reads configuration files, dislays configuration controls and writes changes
        back to disk.</para>
    </introduction>

   
            
            
<section>
  <title>Prerequisites</title>
  <content>
    <para>Navigate to the <localUri>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</localUri> folder. Unzip <localUri>SchedulerSamples.zip</localUri> in the Samples folder.</para>
  </content>
</section>
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using the Command Prompt</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open the Visual Studio 2008 Command Prompt window and navigate to the <localUri>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</localUri> folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Type <command>msbuild SchedulerConsole.sln</command> at the command line.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>
       
                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using Visual Studio</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open Windows Explorer and navigate to the <localUri>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</localUri>
                            folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Double-click the <localUri>SchedulerConsole.sln</localUri> to open the file in Visual Studio.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            In the <legacyBold>Build</legacyBold> menu, select <legacyBold>Build Solution</legacyBold>.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To run the sample</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        Follow these steps to run the sample application:
                        <list class="ordered">
                            <listItem>Open a <application>Command Prompt</application> or <application>Windows Explorer</application> and navigate to the folder that contains the new
                                executable. <para/>
                                This will usually be <localUri>Samples\SchedulerConsole\bin\Debug</localUri>
                              </listItem>
                            <listItem>Run the application by typing or double-clicking:
                            <code language="other">
                            SchedulerConfiguration.exe
                            </code>
                            </listItem>
                        </list>
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>Requirements</title>
  <content>
    <para>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1 and .NET Framework 2.0.</para>
  </content>
</section>

            
            
<section>
  <title>Remarks</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        This sample demonstrates the use of the .NET configuration system to read and write configuration files. The sample also demonstrates how simple UI controls
                        can be set up to modify scheduler setup.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                      Using the <codeEntityReference qulifyHint="true">T:DemiCode.Scheduling.Configuration.SchedulerSettingsManager</codeEntityReference> we can access the configuration object model loaded from a given .config file.
                      <code language="c#">
private void LoadSettings()
{
    SchedulerSettings settings = SchedulerSettingsManager.GetConfigurationSection(&quot;...path to .config file&quot;, &quot;scheduling&quot;);
    SchedulerElement scheduler = settings.Schedulers[settings.DefaultScheduler];

    foreach (ScheduleElementBase schedule in scheduler)
    {
        // Examine schedule elements here
        ...
    }
}
</code>
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        Again, using the <codeEntityReference qulifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Scheduling.Configuration.SchedulerSettingsManager</codeEntityReference>, we can open a configuration section for writing. The configuration object model can then be modified, e.g. according to user selections,
                        and written back to disk. The following sample illustrates how we can open an existing .config file, modify the object model, and then write
                        back to the same file.
                        <code language="c#">
private void SaveSettings()
{
    SchedulerSettings settings = SchedulerSettingsManager.GetWritableConfigurationSection(&quot;scheduling&quot;, &quot;...path to .config file&quot;);
    SchedulerElement defaultScheduler = settings.Schedulers[settings.DefaultScheduler];

    // Modify the object model here
    IntervalScheduleElement schedule = new IntervalScheduleElement("interval", DateTime.Now, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
    defaultScheduler.Add(schedule);
    
    SchedulerSettingsManager.SaveConfigurationSection(settings);
}
                        </code>
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

            
        
    

